o (USP), established in 1934 in São Paulo, Brazil, stands as a beacon of higher education and research in South America. It emerged during a critical period of modernization and industrialization in Brazil, aimed at fostering intellectual growth and addressing societal needs. The university was founded through the amalgamation of several existing institutions, significantly influenced by key figures such as Francisco Campos, who served as its first rector, and the intellectual movements of the early 20th century that emphasized the importance of education for national development.

